<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Head to Toes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple responses to texts. Interactive: Write simple words supported with drawings to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple sentence responses to questions about body parts and clothing (e.g., What do you wear on your head? "I wear a hat."). Can write simple words about body parts supported with drawings to communicate ideas (e.g., drawing a hand and writing "hand" to label it). Can write, with support, simple phrases or sentences about personal experiences with body parts or clothing (e.g., "I wear my raincoat on rainy days.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f0f0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage engages students, activates prior knowledge, and sets the context for the lesson through interactive activities and clear language modeling.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in "Simon Says Body Parts and Clothing". The teacher gives commands using imperatives + nouns and actions: "Simon says touch your nose!", "Simon says put on your hat!", "Simon says bend your knee!". Students follow if "Simon says" is used. This revises vocabulary (head, toes, hand, foot, arm, leg, knee, eye, nose, mouth, ear, hat, shoes, coat, pants, socks) and grammar (Imperative + noun).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing the word for a body part or clothing item, for example, drawing a simple hand on the board and writing "hand" next to it. Then, the teacher models a simple sentence response. Teacher points to a picture of a head with a hat and asks, "What do I wear on my head?" and writes "I wear a hat." on the board, pointing to each word as it's written. Pronunciation of /h/ in "hat" and "hand" is highlighted, and initial sounds are identified.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a foot. "Is this a hand or a foot?" (Expected: "A foot!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a boy putting on a shirt. "Are we going to draw or write today?" (Expected: "Write!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are presented with new language or content relevant to the scenario through authentic or semi-authentic materials, engaging their comprehension skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</b> The teacher displays a big poster titled "My Body and My Clothes" with clear, simple drawings of body parts and clothing items. Each drawing has a simple label (e.g., "This is my arm.", "This is a shirt."). The poster also includes simple sentences like "I wear socks on my feet." or "My jacket is warm." using present simple, demonstratives (this), and possessive adjectives (my).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated poster with simple descriptive sentences and labels. Genre: Informational chart. Register: Simple and direct. Format: Large print with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing using simple present tense and possessive adjectives; understanding simple descriptive statements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a drawing of a 'leg' on the poster. "Is this a foot or a leg?" Students point to the correct word on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "What do I wear on my hands?" (pointing to a picture of hands). Students point to "socks" or "gloves" (if available, otherwise "socks"). Teacher points to a picture of "socks" and asks "Where do I wear socks?" (Expected: "On my feet!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match written words (e.g., "head", "hat") to corresponding pictures on a mini-worksheet, drawing a line from word to picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in writing through controlled and semi-controlled activities, allowing students to practice the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple words for body parts and clothing. Writing simple sentences using "This is my..." and "I wear... on my...". Grammar: Present simple (This is, I wear), possessive adjectives (my, your). Vocabulary: head, hand, foot, arm, leg, hat, shoes, socks, jacket, pants.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Labeling):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple outlines of body parts (e.g., hand, foot, arm, head, leg) and clothing items (e.g., hat, shoe, sock, pants). Students draw lines from a word bank at the bottom of the page (e.g., "hand", "foot", "hat", "socks") to the correct picture and then write the word to label each drawing. (Focus: writing simple words supported with drawings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive another worksheet with cloze sentences. Each sentence has a picture prompt. For example: a picture of a hand and a blank line, "This is my ______." (Expected: hand). A picture of a hat on a head and a blank, "I wear a ______ on my head." (Expected: hat). Students complete the sentences with the correct vocabulary word. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Sentence Writing:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (e.g., "shoe", "pants", "jacket") and sentence starter cards (e.g., "This is my...", "I wear... on my..."). Students pick a picture card, then write a simple sentence about it on a small whiteboard or in their notebooks. For example, if they pick "pants", they might write "I wear pants." or "These are my pants." They then show their written sentence to their partner. The teacher encourages peer-checking for readability and correct word use.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this stage, students apply their language skills to a realistic communicative task, emphasizing fluency and collaborative interaction to complete the task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</b> Students will create a "My Body & My Clothes" mini-booklet or poster page.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual writing and drawing project, presented to a small group of peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme: "Head to Toes!" and Scenario: "All About My Body".</b> Each student receives blank paper (folded into a small booklet or a single poster page). They are instructed to draw at least three body parts (e.g., hand, foot, head) and three clothing items (e.g., hat, shoes, shirt) and write a simple sentence or label for each. For example, they draw a hand and write "This is my hand." or draw a shirt and write "I wear a shirt." They can also respond to a question like "What do you wear on your head?" by drawing a hat and writing "I wear a hat." The teacher reminds them to use the vocabulary learned and simple sentences (present simple, possessive adjectives). Students share their mini-booklet/poster with a peer, pointing to their drawings and reading their sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> While the primary focus is writing, students practice reading their own writing to a peer, demonstrating their ability to communicate ideas through written words and drawings. Peer interaction focuses on understanding each other's written output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment helps gauge student learning, while self-assessment encourages metacognition and reflection on their progress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production stage and review of student mini-booklets/posters. Focus on written output for clarity and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Body & My Clothes" mini-booklet/poster page.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear drawings supported by simple written words/labels (e.g., drawing a hand and writing "hand").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (body parts and clothing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to write simple sentences (e.g., "This is my arm.", "I wear shoes.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort and completion of the task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one body part word you wrote today?" (e.g., hand)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one clothing word you wrote today?" (e.g., hat)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage provides an opportunity for students and the teacher to reflect on the learning process, identify areas for improvement, and connect to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard to write today? (e.g., a long word, a sentence)"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., visual prompts, sentence frames were very effective for 2nd graders to produce written output).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 5 - Mediation & Integration)? (e.g., students struggled with spelling longer words like "shoulder" or "comfortable"; consider providing word banks or dictation for specific words in future activities. Ensure enough time for individual writing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage students to use descriptive adjectives in their writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The written outputs from this lesson (mini-booklets/posters) will be used as a basis for the next lesson's mediation task, where students might describe their own drawings/writing to a different peer or family member, or categorize clothing based on weather, consolidating their understanding and applying their 21st-century skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>